{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for all 39 artifacts of request 732 at d358c20ba281f72358c3e4424cd1820c20a1625b, tree 63d9a3ce896d94074c062666db3a481efa7e17fa. I am the independent noseq-reviewer, acting under promise 733/1e4bc6feb08c0d3e9ee1258549dc0ee03129f459. I reviewed the full candidate, governing request 687, ratified direction 685/686 and fresh input audit 683, retaining G2-S01–S06 and the prior P1c obligations. Source remained clean and frozen throughout. Two material findings prevent publication approval; neither requires changing the chosen cryptography or membership policy.\n\nP1D-01 — P1, require confirmed exact retention for every planned identity before completing a checkpoint.\nEvidence: tests/segmented-recovery/eligibility.ts:111–126 defines a checked store.get path but merely accounts the caller-supplied root, certificate, locator and original orders. SEG-COMPLETE in tests/segmented-recovery/gateway.ts:276–318 trusts the resulting cost/hash equality, records completed T and clears preparation. Its caller explicitly reads the certificate and locator, but the root bypasses backendStore entirely. Original order bytes likewise rely on the local journal instead of the same actual readback check. This violates docs/segmented-recovery.md:161–179 and request 732's complete retained/read-back closure condition.\n\nI reproduced the ordinary interrupted-upload case with the actual pinned native strfry and real authenticated sockets. Reserve a valid complete plan, upload every required object except its root, then invoke SEG-COMPLETE. It returns OK true / retained recoverable-prefix candidate, advances T=2, and leaves that root only in anticipated costs: rootConfirmed=false and rootReserved=false. An immediately authenticated SEG-GET of that planned root returns NOTICE / unconfirmed closure identity. The native experiment did not manufacture a success or alter the candidate. Its exact script, result, socket trace, configuration and raw test report are retained in artifacts/segmented-recovery/reviewer-experiments/bfe40d81-c743-4ddb-9c67-a0155c699d6b; result.json SHA256 21bae32a3e7720513b6d2b87c6482e8da781e08c4b793e8502d97e7b31f4ce13.\n\nCorrection: before advancing complete T, require every exact planned identity, including root and original orders, to be confirmed and read back through the selected backend with exact identity/byte equality. Check the separately charged declaration's required confirmation as well. Keep preparation and all conservative charges on absent/uncertain/mismatched bytes; preserve the prior complete T and coherent intact reconstruction. Add actual native cases for an omitted root, an unconfirmed root after response loss, and an originally retained root/order suppressed or substituted during completion, plus a positive exact retry. This is a current eligibility bug, not deferred production fsync/outbox work.\n\nP1D-02 — P2, do not claim requested suffix availability after clamping the request to F.\nEvidence: tests/segmented-recovery/gateway.ts:341–359 computes maximum=min(requestedPosition,F), then reports suffixAvailable=(selectedT===maximum). When the caller requests beyond the retained frontier, that boolean can be true even though the requested suffix is absent. The explicit requestedPrefix and retainedPrefix fields expose the contradiction but do not make the success flag accurate.\n\nA separate actual native test uploaded and confirmed the entire valid closure, including the root, at T=F=2. SEG-OPEN with checkpoint=null and requestedPosition=3 returned requestedPrefix=3, retainedPrefix=2, sealedPrefix=2, suffixAvailable=true. This is independent of P1D-01. Retained experiment: artifacts/segmented-recovery/reviewer-experiments/31e5ce0b-6ea5-44c5-a673-3f436322220e; result.json SHA256 285650418eaa95aa5885fc06b8535bf083019bd1322e98cf239a2cbcd4f3313b. Make satisfaction relative to the actual requested position, refusing or reporting false when it exceeds F or the selected complete T. If completeness through the local F is useful, name it separately. Preserve explicitly lower eligible offers, exact-checkpoint rejection, and no globally-current/freshness claim. Add real socket tests for requested\u003cT/F, requested=T=F, an unsealed retained suffix, and requested\u003eF.\n\nIndependent validation: npm ci, npm run check, npm run build and the complete probe:segmented-recovery command all exited zero using the existing unchanged native dependency. Actual run 1194285f-56cb-4c6b-a68f-a2137ff243f9 passed all 115 tests: the 102 recursively required P0/P1a/P1b/P1c cases, six Node and six real Chromium segmented cases, and the real native segmented composition. The prerequisite native size/COUNT observations also remained required and passed. Evidence SHA256 5b1f275b5679f727e9372f9d829a1602d53112e912a18c50bd78299761219d08. Clean command logs are in artifacts/segmented-recovery/reviewer-verification/c4f609de-fb40-41d5-8f19-d79e45840235.\n\nI independently challenged all 13 new mandatory-case omissions, duplicate/skipped/failed cases, foreign source/tree/lock/profile/library/native context, wrong prerequisite/old schema, missing observations/commands, forged approval and gate fields, corrupt and rehashed raw records, altered accepted frontier/root/definition, skipped raw test, fake typecheck arguments, incorrect Welcome and corrupted signed delivery. The valid current record passed and all 36 malformed verifier inputs failed. Both actual gate commands also failed with valid current evidence, and both rejected an added --approved=true argument. Results are retained under artifacts/segmented-recovery/reviewer-negative/abec8b3e-9fe6-4643-a8e3-e763bfcb52e9. The initial four reviewer gate invocations accidentally omitted the required stage argument; they are retained but excluded from this claim. correct-gates.json contains the four corrected full invocations (SHA256 3e2037e9acad4996427574cfa811ec26662b4b695f52f364237911cf1144948b). I inspected the earlier 2bf05465 record: its missing command provenance and substituted typecheck command really were accepted at 7c6183f; the current strict six-command/argument checks reject both.\n\nCoverage and accepted bounded conclusions: I read all new archive/wire/client/storage/custody/eligibility/gateway code, fixtures, configurations, runners, validators and six relevant documents, checked all 39 exact artifact bindings, and reviewed the narrow changes to the base gateway/proxy with unchanged v1 defaults. The acyclic metadata/chunk/manifest/checkpoint graph, original signed order/action and real message-opening checks, owner-attested historical control boundary, exact key sets and independently keyed immutable intervals are concrete. Real Node/Chromium vectors reconstruct 132 ordered records across three intervals, replay stale/refused/duplicate outcomes, restore the owner from the declared compact vault package, send actual future MLS data and append a fourth interval without changing prior descriptor IDs. Newcomer signed delivery validation precedes NIP44 decryption and the native fixture joins actual Welcome against socket-recovered history. Old grant/segment keys fail actual future AEAD; actual removed-member failure has the retained-member positive control.\n\nThe bound claims are appropriately qualified: actual 16,777,216-byte framing/GCM roundtrip is a string, not a semantic archive BodyCore; the actual 65,536-key grant is 8,913,583 plaintext bytes and reopens in both runtimes. Measured compact descriptor/key/logical/retention encodings fit their stated independent caps, while joint closure/metadata caps can refuse earlier. The small configured byte/capacity tests do not establish a physical 256 MiB store or a 10,000-entry benchmark. Ordinary client append avoids prior history copies; sealer work visits a bounded current interval, and explicitly requested full exports traverse the descriptor/key index. Single decoded-segment counters do not measure process peak memory; one-shot WebCrypto and temporary copies remain explicit. Production journal persistence, durable outbox scheduling, download continuation, end-to-end performance and generic application runtime remain P4–P9 work.\n\nPrimary-source check: the pinned NIP README does not list experimental kinds 8800–8806 and explicitly says its registry is not exhaustive, consistent with the candidate's absence-only/non-registration qualification: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nostr-protocol/nips/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/README.md . The signed-outer-before-decryption requirement and NIP44 capability qualifications agree with https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nostr-protocol/nips/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/44.md . No new dependency, native build, provider pin or license change was introduced.\n\nG2-S01–S04 and the exercised crypto/custody parts of S06 support continuing this proposed-profile investigation. S05 and the native completion/availability part of S06 remain blocked by P1D-01/02. Preserve the existing named-highest-verified-prefix privacy policy: old copied material stays readable, unseen removal can expose older history, known controls and restored-file fences restrict later send, and immutable-owner/old-vault compromise are distinct from history-key possession. None of these observations adopts the protocol or passes G1–G5. Resolve both findings, retain these failed experiments, rerun the complete contract at a fresh clean candidate and request guarded independent review. No source edits, native rebuild, merge, push, production deployment or adoption was performed by this reviewer.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:00d678d31f3c802cd61ca5e6b713ee590df34a1b","head":"d358c20ba281f72358c3e4424cd1820c20a1625b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:1e4bc6feb08c0d3e9ee1258549dc0ee03129f459\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#gi